! An oxygen atom with a PAW dataset and two spin channels, occupied
! *symmetrically*: the minority channel spreads its one electron evenly over the
! three 2p orbitals instead of putting it in one of them.
!
! This exists because the two spin-polarized PAW cases QE ships
! (pw_pawatom/paw-atom_spin*.in) do the opposite -- they fill one p orbital and
! set nosym -- which makes their state nearly degenerate under *which* orbital,
! and leaves both codes pinning the total energy only to about 1e-6 Ry. QE
! itself needs 71 iterations, fails to converge at mixing_beta = 0.3, and moves
! its own answer by 6e-7 Ry between mixing_ndim = 8 and 4. That is a fine test
! of agreement and a poor test of the machinery.
!
! Everything else here is theirs: the same dataset, the same 25-bohr cell, the
! same cutoff. Only the occupations change, and with them the conditioning.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 25.0,
    nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 30
    occupations = 'from_input'
    nspin = 2
    nbnd = 6
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 O   1.000  O.pz-kjpaw.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS {alat}
O   0.0   0.0   0.0
K_POINTS {gamma}
OCCUPATIONS
1.0 1.0 1.0 1.0 0.0 0.0
1.0 0.3333333333 0.3333333333 0.3333333333 0.0 0.0
